Text 2: Historian Tomasz Krawczyk maintains that the Republic''s collapse resulted from institutional rigidity. The constitutional framework designed for a small city-state could not accommodate the administrative demands of a Mediterranean empire. Krawczyk argues that even the most equitable wealth distribution could not have prevented collapse if governing institutions remained unable to manage territories spanning three continents.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 2 identifies a different primary cause for the same historical outcome discussed in Text 1."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 2 provides additional evidence supporting the argument made in Text 1."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 2 examines a later historical period than Text 1 to test the same hypothesis."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 2 applies the economic framework introduced in Text 1 to a different civilization."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Both texts address the same event—the decline of the Roman Republic—but attribute it to different causes: Text 1 emphasizes economic inequality while Text 2 emphasizes institutional rigidity. The choice about identifying a different primary cause for the same outcome captures this relationship. The choice about providing additional evidence for Text 1''s argument is wrong because Text 2 explicitly offers a competing explanation, not supporting evidence. The choice about examining a later historical period is wrong because both texts discuss the same era of the Roman Republic. The choice about applying Text 1''s economic framework to a different civilization is wrong because Text 2 discusses the same civilization and uses an institutional framework rather than an economic one.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, 