Text 2: Computer scientist Rajan Subramanian proposes that understanding should be evaluated functionally rather than by reference to internal mechanisms. If an AI system can consistently generate contextually appropriate responses, answer novel questions accurately, and transfer knowledge across domains, Subramanian argues, then the system satisfies any operationally meaningful definition of understanding—regardless of whether its internal processes resemble human cognition.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"As defining understanding in a way that conflates useful behavioral outputs with genuine comprehension of meaning."},{"key":"B","text":"As proposing a test that would be impossible to administer to current artificial intelligence systems."},{"key":"C","text":"As overlooking the practical benefits that AI systems provide even without possessing true understanding."},{"key":"D","text":"As offering a framework that, while theoretically interesting, applies only to future AI systems not yet developed."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 1 argues that producing correct outputs through pattern matching does not constitute genuine understanding—the clerk analogy explicitly separates systematic useful behavior from actual knowledge of content. Text 2''s functional definition equates consistent, appropriate behavioral outputs with understanding. From Matsuda''s perspective, this functional criterion mistakes the appearance of comprehension (systematic, useful behavior) for genuine semantic grasp, which is exactly what her clerk analogy warns against. This matches the choice about conflating useful behavioral outputs with genuine comprehension. The choice about proposing an impossible test is wrong because Text 1 does not question whether the functional criteria can be measured—it questions whether meeting them constitutes understanding. The choice about overlooking practical benefits is wrong because Text 1 acknowledges that pattern matching can be "systematic and often useful"; her objection is about what counts as understanding, not about practical utility. The choice about applying only to future systems is wrong because Text 2 frames its argument as applicable to current systems, and Text 1''s critique is conceptual rather than temporal.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Text 2: Environmental engineer Priya Nair notes that while green roofs offer measurable thermal and hydrological benefits, their effectiveness depends heavily on local climate conditions. In arid regions with limited rainfall, green roofs require substantial irrigation, which can offset their environmental advantages and increase net water consumption.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By agreeing that green roofs are beneficial but noting that their advantages are not universal across all climates."},{"key":"B","text":"By rejecting the idea that green roofs provide any meaningful environmental benefit."},{"key":"C","text":"By arguing that green roofs should be mandated on residential buildings rather than commercial ones."},{"key":"D","text":"By claiming that stormwater management is a more important consideration than reducing heat island effects."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 2 acknowledges that green roofs offer "measurable thermal and hydrological benefits" but qualifies that their effectiveness varies by climate, particularly in arid regions where irrigation can negate advantages. This is a partial agreement with a climate-based caveat, matching the choice about agreeing green roofs are beneficial while noting their advantages are not universal. The choice about rejecting any meaningful benefit is wrong because Text 2 explicitly affirms that green roofs have measurable benefits. The choice about mandating roofs on residential rather than commercial buildings is wrong because Text 2 does not address building type at all. The choice about prioritizing stormwater management over heat island effects is wrong because Text 2 does not rank one benefit above another.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, fal